# Erdos Problem #99 - Target B coarea-grain Mantel concentration

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Run type:** exact extremal aggregation after boundary-order phase coarea  
**Target:** multi-grain local-to-global concentration  
**Depends on:** phase-coarea boundary-order grain decomposition PCA2-PCA3; triangle-free train crossing graph; `R/D^2 -> Delta_2`; `T=O(D)`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN

## 1. Setup

Fix a small phase-bin diameter `eta>0` and use PCA2 to delete only

`O_eta(D)`

train crossings/rhombi. Let the surviving train-crossing components be

`H_1,...,H_m`,

with

`v_j=|V(H_j)|`, `e_j=|E(H_j)|`.

The deleted crossing count is lower order on the quadratic scale, so if `R` is the original pure-rhombic crossing count,

`R_*:=sum_j e_j=R-o(D^2)`

and

`R_*/D^2 -> Delta_2=pi/(2sqrt(3))`.                     (1.1)

The component train vertex sets are disjoint, hence

`sum_j v_j <= T`.                                       (1.2)

Define crossing-mass fractions

`theta_j=e_j/R_*`,

so

`theta_j>=0`, `sum_j theta_j=1`.                        (1.3)

## 2. Componentwise Mantel sums globally

The full train-crossing graph is triangle-free, so every component is triangle-free. Mantel gives

`e_j<=v_j^2/4`,

hence

`v_j>=2sqrt(e_j)`.                                      (2.1)

Summing and using (1.2),

`T>=2 sum_j sqrt(e_j)`

`=2sqrt(R_*) sum_j sqrt(theta_j)`.                      (2.2)

Therefore:

### Theorem GMC1 - grain concentration entropy bound

If

`x=T/D`,

then

`boxed{
 sum_j sqrt(theta_j)
 <= x/(2sqrt(Delta_2))+o(1).
}`                                                       (2.3)

This is a direct global constraint on the distribution of physical rhombi among coherent coarea grains. No component minimum degree is used.

## 3. Exact largest-grain bound below the two-grain threshold

Let

`Theta=max_j theta_j`.

Suppose

`x<2sqrt(2Delta_2)-epsilon`                             (3.1)

along a subsequence, for some fixed `epsilon>0`. Then the right side of (2.3) is strictly below `sqrt(2)`.

In particular `Theta>1/2+o(1)`: if every grain had mass at most one half, the minimum possible value of `sum sqrt(theta_j)` subject to `sum theta_j=1` is `sqrt(2)`, attained by two equal grains. Splitting mass further only increases the concave-square-root sum.

When `Theta>=1/2`, the minimum `sum sqrt(theta_j)` for fixed largest mass `Theta` is obtained by putting all remaining mass into one second grain. Thus

`sum_j sqrt(theta_j) >= sqrt(Theta)+sqrt(1-Theta)`.     (3.2)

Put

`S=x/(2sqrt(Delta_2))`.                                 (3.3)

For `1<=S<sqrt(2)`, inequality

`sqrt(Theta)+sqrt(1-Theta) <= S`                        (3.4)

with `Theta>=1/2` is equivalent to

`Theta(1-Theta) <= ((S^2-1)/2)^2`.                     (3.5)

Hence:

### Theorem GMC2 - explicit dominant-grain fraction

For every bounded subsequential value

`2sqrt(Delta_2)<=x<2sqrt(2Delta_2)`,

the largest coarea grain satisfies

`boxed{
Theta
>=
1/2 [1+sqrt(1-(S^2-1)^2)]-o(1),
qquad
S=x/(2sqrt(Delta_2)).
}`                                                       (3.6)

The threshold is

`2sqrt(2Delta_2)=2.6935473741...`.                      (3.7)

## 4. Numerical value at the current Section-39 lower edge

The canonical Section-39 lower bound is

`x>=2.5395008183...`.

At exactly this coefficient,

`S=4/3`

(up to the displayed numerical precision), and (3.6) gives

`Theta>=0.8142696805...-o(1)`.                          (4.1)

Thus a survivor whose train count is near the current minimum cannot distribute its physical rhombi among several comparable grains: one coherent phase grain carries at least about `81.4%` of the entire quadratic crossing mass.

## 5. General capped-mass form

For completeness, GMC1 gives a concentration bound above the two-grain threshold as well.

Fix `0<q<=1` and suppose every grain has `theta_j<=q`. Among probability vectors with this cap, the minimum of `sum sqrt(theta_j)` is obtained by filling as many coordinates as possible to `q`, with one final remainder.

Write

`r=floor(1/q)`,

`u=1-rq`, `0<=u<q`.

Then

`sum_j sqrt(theta_j) >= r sqrt(q)+sqrt(u)`.             (5.1)

Therefore any proposed global grain cap `q` must satisfy

`r sqrt(q)+sqrt(1-rq)
<= x/(2sqrt(Delta_2))+o(1)`.                            (5.2)

This form can be combined with future local theorems excluding grains above some fixed crossing-mass fraction.

## 6. Relation to the explicit local phase exclusions

The explicit unique-max/balanced/two-max/GPI reports currently assume that one coherent component carries the global quadratic rhombus core at its scale. GMC2 does not reach that hypothesis: `81.4%` is not `1-o(1)`.

However it turns the multi-grain problem into a quantitative interface problem rather than an arbitrary decomposition. In the low-`T/D` range, only a small residual fraction of the rhombi can lie outside the dominant grain.

A useful next theorem would be a **fractional dominant-component version** of the trimmed-grid phase exclusion:

> if one coherent component carries fraction `Theta` of all rhombi, derive a diameter contradiction for `Theta>Theta_crit(s,t,Delta)` rather than requiring `Theta->1`.

The grid lower bounds in the existing explicit phase reports already have enough numerical margin to make `Theta_crit<1` in several phase-ratio regimes. GMC2 can then be compared directly with that threshold.

## 7. What remains

GMC1-GMC2 do not settle the range `x>=2.693547...`, where two equal quadratic grains are compatible with componentwise Mantel. Nor do they by themselves exclude the `~81%` dominant grain near the present lower edge.

The next concrete route is:

1. rewrite the existing complete-grid diameter proofs with a global rhombus fraction parameter `Theta`;
2. obtain explicit `Theta_crit<1` for balanced and near-balanced phase classes;
3. combine with GMC2 to eliminate a low-`x` interval;
4. use PC3/MCP and MG3-MG4 for the remaining higher-train/multi-grain phase.

## Handoff

```text
After the O_eta(D) phase-coarea cut, let theta_j be the fraction of retained rhombi in grain j.
Componentwise Mantel gives

  T >= 2 sqrt(R_*) sum_j sqrt(theta_j).

Hence

  sum_j sqrt(theta_j)
    <= (T/D)/(2 sqrt(Delta_2)) + o(1).

If x=T/D<2sqrt(2Delta_2)=2.693547..., the largest grain fraction obeys

  Theta >= 1/2[1+sqrt(1-(x^2/(4Delta_2)-1)^2)]-o(1).

At x=2.5395008183..., Theta>=0.8142696805... .

This is the first quantitative crossing-mass concentration theorem for the boundary-order coherent grain decomposition.
```
